#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mathieu_s

#% text
# `mathieu_s(a, q, z)` gives the odd Mathieu function with characteristic value `a` and parameter `q`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is the odd Floquet solution `se_nu(z) = sum_k c_k sin((nu + 2k) z)` of `y'' + (a - 2 q cos(2 z)) y = 0` with `sum c_k^2 = 1` (DLMF 28.2, 28.12); it is odd in `z`.

#% item
# Numerical values: Taylor series of the equation re-expanded along the segment from 0, and the normalization from the characteristic exponent `nu` (`cos(pi nu) = w_I(pi)`, DLMF 28.2.16) and the Fourier coefficients of the Floquet solution (continued fractions, `sum c_k^2 = 1`). Real `a` and `q` use this normalization throughout; complex `a` or `q` use the principal branches and may differ from other conventions by the normalization.

#% item
# `mathieu_s(a, 0, z) = sin(sqrt(a) z)/sqrt(a)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
mathieu_s(2, 1, 3.2)
#% output
#= -0.7680982888758993

#% cell
n(mathieu_s(2, 1, 37/10), 20)
#% output
#= -0.948256749594045741607081

#% cell
mathieu_s(a, q, -x)
#% output
#= -mathieu_s(a, q, x)

#% cell
mathieu_s(2, 1, 0)
#% output
#= 0

